# Deferred — do not implement yet

## D1 — Shared sentinels (deferred)

A tareth form authorizes using one instance's sentinel set to validate another
instance. This is context-dependent. Do not implement a universal mechanism.

Only implement when a specific test case exists that would fail if shared
sentinels were absent. The test must assert: using instance A's sentinels for
instance B produces a different (wrong) outcome vs using instance-specific
sentinels, and the form cert correctly identifies when sharing is safe.

## D2 — Shared compression (deferred)

Same rule as D1. A tareth form authorizes applying a compression discovered on
one instance to other instances. Do not implement until a test certifies it.

## D3 — Instance routing (deferred)

Routing new instances to a form's context using form priors. Do not implement
until a test would fail without it.

## D5 — Var-level route certs (deferred — completes the taxonomy's core rule for vars)

T1.4 uses skip cert as proxy for route eligibility. This is explicitly partial.
The taxonomy's core rule (`trass-for-skip ≠ trass-for-route`) is only implemented
for forms after T0–T5. For vars, skip and route are the same value.

A var-level route cert answers: "is this var a valid routing target for hypothesis
enumeration?" That is a different question from "does perturbing this var propagate
downstream?" A var could be tareth-for-skip (its perturbation matters) but
trass-for-route (it is not a useful parent candidate for other vars' hypotheses,
e.g. because it is always downstream, never upstream in the fitted structure).

Only implement when a test exists that would fail without it — i.e., a case where
a var is tareth-for-skip but should be excluded from parent selection, or
trass-for-skip but should still be a valid parent candidate. Until that case
exists in a test, the proxy is sufficient and honest.

## D4 — TiedFrontier consumers (deferred)

The old TODO (P1–P7) covers TiedFrontier work. That work is still valid but
depends on the taxonomy gaps (T0–T5) being resolved first. Do not activate
frontier-biased probes or collapse gating until operation-scoped certs exist,
because the frontier consumers will need to read scoped certs to make correct
decisions.

